.Dd November 06, 2026
.Dt REUI 3
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm reui
.Nd portable ui toolkit
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In reui/reui.h
.In reui/util.h

.Ft bool
.Fn reui_setup "void"
.Ft void
.Fn reui_teardown "void"
.Ft Win
.Fn win_new "const char* title" "int x" "int y"
.Ft bool
.Fn win_shouldclose "Win* win"
.Ft void
.Fn win_kill "Win* to_kill"
.\" NOTE: keep this list of functions updated!
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is a portable UI toolkit that render native-looking applications. It abstracts
OS UI toolkit with a simple, C-native, easy to use API.
.Pp
.Nm
provides few abstractions and is deliberately low-level, while the
.Sy reui/util.h
header provides small utilities to ease developing applications with the
library. The development interface is split into the following abstractions:

.Bl
.It
A widget is a graphical, interactive element ultimately displayed on the
window. It holds a set of private state and is rendered by the backend.

.It
.Ft MesgMap
    Associates events to functions (named call-back functions) to call back
using the
.Fn fire_messages
function. Callback functions take two arguments: an object representing the
widget where the event took place, and the userdata argument, which is of type
.Ft void*
and can be set for each association individually. This is usually used for
passing application state without globals (which is considered a good general
practice and avoids bugs in multi-threaded applications).
.It
.Ft UIMap
    Stores a representation of the widgets. This representation is used by the
backend to render the specified widgets.
.Ft UIMap
only stores references to these widgets, freeing an
.Ft UIMap
does
.Sy NOT
free the widgets it holds. Think of it as more of a "register" of the UI
widgets, not a place to store widgets.

